<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diogo Cão</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1452–c. 1486</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diogo Cão was a Portuguese navigator who explored the coast of west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the 1480s. He reached the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> around 1483, establishing the first contact between Portugal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a>. This contact led to diplomatic exchanges, the conversion of the Kongo elite to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and long-term Portuguese involvement in the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diogo-cao.jpg" alt="Diogo Cão" width="150" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Portuguese National Mint · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diogo_C%C3%A3o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
